body.translated_text:
8.1ab: Now I shall expound the worship (<s>yajana</s>) that grants all desires,
8.1cd: by merely seeing which one becomes approved by the <s>Yoginīs</s>.
8.2ab: Therein, first, one should construct a beautiful worship hall (<s>yāgasadana</s>) in an auspicious and pleasant field,
8.2cd: furnished with a circular fire-pit (<s>agnikuṇḍa</s>),
8.3ab: with twenty-five divisions, surrounded by a half-navel [rim],
8.3cd: with a belt of one-quarter [width], and with a well-formed division-lip.
8.4ab: Then, having bathed, having conquered the pairs [of opposites], the knower of <s>mantras</s> [should perform] the inner bath (<s>bhāvasnāna</s>);
8.4cd: that is taught as sixfold, in the sequence beginning with the ash-bath (<s>bhasmasnāna</s>).
8.5ab: The ash-bath [is performed] with the great weapon-<s>mantra</s> (<s>mahāstra</s>): ash consecrated seven times;
8.5cd: for the removal of impurity, one should dust the body in the order of dissolution (<s>saṃhārakrama</s>).
8.6ab: Then with the five limbs of the <s>vidyā</s>, one should veil [the body] beginning from the head;
8.6cd: the consecration (<s>abhiṣeka</s>) should be performed with the root [<s>mantra</s>] itself, along with the six limbs.
8.7ab: Then, without garments or well-clothed, one should wash the hands and feet;
8.7cd: having sipped water (<s>ācamana</s>), one should perform purification (<s>mārjana</s>) with the <s>vidyā</s> of many syllables.
8.8ab: Having performed the general <s>nyāsa</s> (placement), [one should recite] the <s>aghamarṣa</s> (sin-destroying hymn) with the second [<s>vidyā</s>];
8.8cd: and the invocation (<s>upasthāna</s>) with [the <s>vidyā</s> of] <s>Mālinī</s>; one should recite the supreme single-syllable [<s>mantra</s>].
8.9ab: In the water-bath (<s>jalasnāna</s>) also, with the weapon-<s>mantra</s>, clay consecrated seven times—
8.9cd: touching the body as before, one should perform the impurity-removing bath.
8.10ab: The ritual bath (<s>vidhisnāna</s>) and so forth here [are] as before, but with water;
8.10cd: having bathed with the ordinary ritual method, water consecrated with the three <s>vidyās</s>
8.11ab: the knower of <s>mantras</s> should cast upon the head for the <s>mantra</s>-bath.
8.11cd: With dust shaken from a cow, one should perform the wind-bath (<s>vāyavyasnāna</s>).
8.12ab: Uttering the great weapon-<s>mantra</s>, one should walk seven steps while meditating;
8.12cd: then return again the same [path], meditating on <s>Parāparā</s>.
8.13ab: The celestial [bath] (<s>divya</s>) is considered to be of such nature through the combination of rain and sunshine;
8.13cd: but therein the <s>mantra</s>-practitioner should meditate on the supreme [<s>Śakti</s>] streaming <s>amṛta</s> (nectar).
8.14ab: With the weapon-<s>mantra</s>, raising fire from the base of the thumb, one should burn
8.14cd: one's own body; then flood [it] with the nectar of the supreme [<s>Śakti</s>].
8.15ab: Taking the <s>mantra</s> at [the time of] the sun and so forth, one should go while meditating on the weapon-<s>mantra</s>;
8.15cd: one should enter the worship hall, purified by the weapon-<s>mantra</s>, pure and undisturbed.
8.16ab: There, having worshipped the door-guardians, with a flower consecrated by the great weapon-<s>mantra</s>,
8.16cd: one should cast [it] while meditating, for the pacification of blazing obstacles.
8.17ab: Then in all ten directions, having resolved upon protection with the weapon-<s>mantra</s>,
8.17cd: one should enter the worship hall, like fire, united with fire.
8.18ab: Facing east or with a gentle face, one should begin the special <s>nyāsa</s>;
8.18cd: therein first, with the weapon-<s>mantra</s>, blazing like the fire of dissolution (<s>kālānala</s>),
8.19ab: one should visualize the body as burned from the tip of the thumb, both externally and internally;
8.19cd: meditating on that ash being scattered by the wind of the armor [<s>mantra</s>],
8.20ab: one should contemplate the self as having the form of <s>Śiva</s>'s <s>bindu</s> (point).
8.20cd: Then one should unite <s>Śakti</s> with this [self], [reciting] 'I am He' (<s>so'ham</s>), the unconquered.
8.21ab: Then one should place the <s>vidyā</s>-form with this <s>mantra</s>, O <s>Śāṅkarī</s>:
8.21cd: the great <s>prāṇa</s> surmounted by the staff (<s>daṇḍa</s>), mounted on the staff, with the navel,
8.22ab: the hips below that, and below again the left breast,
8.22cd: the throat, the left peak, adorned with the left <s>mudrā</s>,
8.23ab: the <s>bindu</s>, half-moon, space, <s>nāda</s>, adorned with <s>Śakti</s>-<s>bindu</s>—
8.23cd: this is called the body-maker (<s>piṇḍakara</s>), O Goddess, known as nine-fold in nature.
8.24ab: This, which grants all <s>siddhis</s> (accomplishments), is proclaimed with its secret;
8.24cd: this [<s>mantra</s>], divested of three syllables below, is joined with six long vowels.
8.25ab: The six limbs (<s>ṣaḍaṅga</s>) beginning with <s>hṛdaya</s> (heart) should be arranged according to the division of class;
8.25cd: with the blazing seed-syllables kṣa, ra, va, la, adorned with <s>bindu</s>.
8.26ab: The faces should first be arranged, beginning with the upper face, in sequence;
8.26cd: for the accomplishment of the subsidiary-limb ritual, one should place [them] on the forehead and so forth.
8.27ab: 'a' on the forehead; the second ('ā') one should place on the mouth;
8.27cd: 'i' and 'ī' placed on the two eyes; 'u' and 'ū' placed on the two ears;
8.28ab: 'ṛ' and 'ṝ' on the two nostrils; likewise 'ḷ' and 'ḹ' on the two cheeks;
8.28cd: 'e' and 'ai' on the upper and lower teeth; 'o' and 'au' on the two lips;
8.29ab: 'aṃ' on the topknot (<s>śikhā</s>); with <s>visarga</s> one should form the tongue.
8.29cd: On the right shoulder, arm, forearm, hand, fingers, and nails
8.30ab: one should place the <s>ka</s>-group; on the left likewise, the first [group] in order.
8.30cd: The <s>ṭa</s>- and <s>ta</s>-groups, as before, one should place on the hips, thighs, and so forth.
8.31ab: The <s>pa</s>-group on the two sides, back, belly, and heart, in order;
8.31cd: the <s>ya</s>-group should be placed on skin, blood, flesh, and sinews.
8.32ab: The five beginning with <s>śa</s> on bone, fat, semen, <s>prāṇa</s> (life-breath), and anger.
8.32cd: Then, having placed the body-limbs, the wise one should invoke <s>Śiva</s>.
8.33ab: Above <s>prāṇa</s> one should place the navel; above that, the right finger;
8.33cd: measured by the left ear, beyond that—<s>Śiva</s>, the bestower of all <s>siddhis</s>.
8.34ab: This is indeed the supreme true nature (<s>sadbhāva</s>) of the great-souled <s>Bhairava</s>;
8.34cd: the limbs should be made with this [<s>mantra</s>], as before, according to vowel-distinctions.
8.35ab: The form (<s>mūrti</s>), creation (<s>sṛṣṭi</s>), the triple <s>tattva</s>, and eight with the body-limbs,
8.35cd: <s>Śiva</s> with his limbs—thus the <s>nyāsa</s> is proclaimed as sixteenfold.
8.36ab: Above this, one should then perform the <s>Śākta</s> (pertaining to <s>Śakti</s>) <s>nyāsa</s>; listen how:
8.36cd: on the form, one should place <s>Parāparā</s>; on its faces, <s>Mālinī</s>;
8.37ab: then the triad beginning with <s>Parā</s>, reaching the topknot, heart, and feet;
8.37cd: [placed] in sequence on the face, throat, heart, navel, secret region, thighs, and feet;
8.38ab: having placed the eight beginning with <s>Aghorā</s>, and the <s>vidyā</s>-limbs as before,
8.38cd: one should then invoke <s>Śakti</s>, worshipped by all <s>yogins</s>.
8.39ab: The <s>jīva</s> (individual soul), situated within the cavity of <s>prāṇa</s>, blazing like the fire of dissolution,
8.39cd: exceedingly brilliant, adorned on the left foot, with <s>bindu</s> on the head,
8.40ab: joined with the right knee, accompanied by all the hosts of <s>Mātṛs</s> (Mothers)—
8.40cd: propitiated by this [<s>mantra</s>], all [the Mothers] grant the desired fruit.
8.41ab: This is proclaimed as the supreme true nature (<s>sadbhāva</s>) of the <s>Mātṛs</s>;
8.41cd: therefore the <s>mantra</s>-practitioner who desires the supreme <s>siddhi</s> should recite this.
8.42ab: The union of <s>Rudra</s> and <s>Śakti</s> is eternally established herein;
8.42cd: since this supreme <s>Śakti</s> is proclaimed through this distinction.
8.43ab: However many <s>siddhis</s> exist in the <s>Tantra</s>, all are accomplished by her;
8.43cd: her limbs should be arranged as before, according to vowel-distinctions.
8.44ab: The form with faces, <s>Śakti</s>, the triad of <s>vidyās</s>,
8.44cd: the eight beginning with <s>Aghorā</s>, and the five <s>vidyā</s>-limbs—
8.45ab: the supreme <s>Śakti</s> with her limbs—thus the <s>nyāsa</s> is declared as sixfold.
8.45cd: This paired (<s>yāmala</s>) <s>nyāsa</s> is for the accomplishment of all <s>siddhis</s>.
8.46ab: This left-handed (<s>vāma</s>) procedure should be performed by those who follow the path of liberation;
8.46cd: or separately, through the distinction of letter-<s>mantras</s>, by those desiring their respective fruits.
8.47ab: As many as have been proclaimed through the distinctions of <s>Śambhu</s>-, <s>Śakti</s>-, and <s>Aṇu</s>-<s>mantras</s>,
8.47cd: in all of these likewise, this <s>nyāsa</s> is considered fivefold.
8.48ab: But whichever external [<s>mantra</s>] is used in a given [context], that should be accompanied by its limbs;
8.48cd: the sixth [variant] would thus arise—hence everywhere this is declared as sixteenfold.
8.49ab: In accordance with one's own practice, through the mental construction of existence and non-existence,
8.49cd: all worship materials (<s>yāgadravya</s>) should be prepared ritually by the wise.
8.50ab: Then, taking the offering-vessel (<s>arghapātra</s>), mentally constructed through existence and non-existence,
8.50cd: then burned by the fire of the weapon-<s>mantra</s>, flooded by the water of <s>Śakti</s>, [made] pure—
8.51ab: whatever substance requires purification,
8.51cd: that should be done by the knowledgeable one through this very method.
8.52ab: Not even the slightest unpurified substance should be used here;
8.52cd: everything purified by this [method], even if impure, becomes pure.
8.53ab: Having filled that [vessel] with water, offering with the six limbs,
8.53cd: having made all [offerings] into <s>amṛta</s> (nectar), one should purify all substances with it.
8.54ab: Having worshipped oneself, one should perform the inner visualization (<s>antaḥkṛti</s>) as follows;
8.54cd: I shall describe it to you, O one worshipped by all hosts of <s>yogins</s>.
8.55ab: First, the supporting <s>Śakti</s> (<s>ādhāraśakti</s>), four finger-breadths below the navel—
8.55cd: earth (<s>dharā</s>), the divine bull (<s>suroda</s>), the boat (<s>pota</s>), and the bulb (<s>kanda</s>)—this fourfold [foundation].
8.56ab: Each of these is one finger-breadth; [with] the trident's pure essence;
8.56cd: then the stalk (<s>nāla</s>), called <s>Ananta</s> (Infinite), one should visualize as the shaft (<s>daṇḍa</s>) of this [trident].
8.57ab: Extending up to the <s>lambikā</s> (uvula), above the trident a knot (<s>granthi</s>) is recognized;
8.57cd: O Great Goddess, without piercing this [knot], the great ocean of the net of bonds—
8.58ab: that person does not attain <s>yoga</s> together with <s>Śiva</s>.
8.58cd: <s>Dharma</s> (righteousness), <s>jñāna</s> (knowledge), <s>vairāgya</s> (dispassion), and <s>aiśvarya</s> (sovereignty)—this fourfold [group]
8.59ab: the <s>mantra</s>-practitioner should visualize at the corners, in the southeastern direction and so forth in order;
8.59cd: and the fourfold group of limbs (<s>gātraka</s>) should be visualized in the directions beginning with the east.
8.60ab: Above the knot, below the trident, there should be a fourfold [arrangement];
8.60cd: they call that the <s>vidyā-tattva</s> (knowledge-principle), joined with three coverings.
8.61ab: Between the soft palate (<s>kakha</s>) and the uvula (<s>lambikā</s>), one should contemplate that <s>tattva</s>;
8.61cd: the wise one should visualize the <s>kakha-tattva</s> as lotus-shaped, [representing] the <s>Īśvara</s> [principle].
8.62ab: Endowed with filaments and pistil, with seed, white, blossoming;
8.62cd: beginning from the eastern petal onward, one should place the nine beginning with <s>Vāmā</s>.
8.63ab: <s>Vāmā</s>, <s>Jyeṣṭhā</s>, <s>Raudrī</s>, <s>Kālī</s>, and <s>Parā</s>,
8.63cd: <s>Kalavikaraṇī</s>, likewise <s>Balavikaraṇī</s>,
8.64ab: <s>Balapramathanī</s>, and also <s>Sarvabhūtadamanī</s>;
8.64cd: <s>Manonmanī</s> in the center—one should place [them] along the path of the sun.
8.65ab: And another group of nine beginning with <s>Vibhu</s>, one should arrange in reverse order:
8.65cd: <s>Vibhu</s>, <s>Jñānī</s>, <s>Kriyā</s>, <s>Icchā</s>, <s>Vāgīśī</s>, <s>Jvālinī</s>,
8.66ab: <s>Vāmā</s>, <s>Jyeṣṭhā</s>, and <s>Raudrī</s>—all blazing like the fire of dissolution;
8.66cd: <s>Brahmā</s>, <s>Viṣṇu</s>, and <s>Hara</s>, who were previously taught as belonging to <s>Śakti</s>,
8.67ab: stationed on the petals, filaments, and center—they are the lords of the <s>maṇḍalas</s> (circles);
8.67cd: one should contemplate them as designated by the sounds of sun, moon, and fire.
8.68ab: And the Lord (<s>Īśvara</s>), the great corpse (<s>mahāpreta</s>), laughing, sentient,
8.68cd: with a body [blazing] like ten million fires of dissolution—thus is the entire seat (<s>āsana</s>).
8.69ab: Arisen from its navel, one should visualize the three points of the <s>Śakti</s>-trident (<s>śūla</s>);
8.69cd: issuing through the three [levels of] <s>kakha</s>, ending at the <s>dvādaśānta</s> (twelve finger-breadths above the head).
8.70ab: On its prongs, one should then visualize three <s>Śakti</s>-lotuses;
8.70cd: the white [lotus], presiding over all—this is the supreme seat.
8.71ab: Above that, one should then contemplate the form called <s>Vidyā</s> (Knowledge);
8.71cd: and then [the form] called <s>Ātman</s> (Self), and upon that, the previous <s>nyāsa</s> of <s>Śiva</s>-nature.
8.72ab: Then in the center, the supreme <s>Śakti</s> (<s>Parā</s>); on the right and left, the two [<s>Śaktis</s>];
8.72cd: <s>Parāparā</s>, in her own form, red-colored, of great power,
8.73ab: bearing the form of will (<s>icchā</s>), to be visualized as somewhat fierce but not terrifying;
8.73cd: <s>Aparā</s> on the left prong, terrifying, black and tawny,
8.74ab: the Goddess bearing the form of will, destroyer of the suffering of the devoted;
8.74cd: the supreme <s>Parā</s>, the nourishing Goddess, with the radiance of ten million moons.
8.75ab: Even when the sixfold <s>Śākta</s> [<s>nyāsa</s>] has been performed, one should still contemplate beginning with the form;
8.75cd: then the five <s>vidyā</s>-limbs should be placed in the southeastern and other directions.
8.76ab: In the directions of Fire, <s>Īśāna</s>, <s>Rakṣas</s> (southwest), and Wind, and to the south, in order,
8.76cd: one should similarly visualize the <s>Śakti</s>-limbs and <s>Śiva</s>-limbs according to the ritual procedure.
8.77ab: But in the directions beginning with <s>Indra</s> (east), [place] the weapon-<s>mantra</s>; in the center, the eye [<s>mantra</s>];
8.77cd: one should visualize the eight beginning with <s>Aghora</s>, accompanied by the eight beginning with <s>Aghorī</s>.
8.78ab: As a surrounding of all [of them], one should visualize the world-protectors (<s>lokapāla</s>s) externally,
8.78cd: with their weapons, with their own <s>mantras</s>; then one should commence <s>japa</s> (recitation).
8.79ab: Having become absorbed in one's own true nature, one should visualize each one tenfold,
8.79cd: contemplating [each] as blazing like fire, and utter [the <s>mantra</s>] ending with <s>svāhā</s>,
8.80ab: once for each, the <s>mantra</s>-practitioner, for the accomplishment of the fire-offering (<s>homa</s>) rite.
8.80cd: Thus has been described the mental worship (<s>mānasa yāga</s>) in its collective form.
8.81ab: This trident has been described: single-shafted, with three <s>Śaktis</s>;
8.81cd: without knowing this <s>Śakti</s>-trident, O beautiful-faced one,
8.82ab: even having bound the <s>khecarī mudrā</s>, one cannot rise from the surface of the earth.
8.82cd: Thus the <s>Śāmbhava</s> (relating to <s>Śiva</s>) [<s>yoga</s>] is said to end in the eighth [level]; the <s>Śākta</s> (relating to <s>Śakti</s>) is considered [to end in the eighth as well];
8.83ab: the <s>Āṇava</s> (relating to the individual soul) should be known as ending in the fourth—thus is the trident of three [levels] considered.
8.83cd: Through the separate worship procedure, one should visualize the <s>Śakti</s>-circle (<s>śakticakra</s>);
8.84ab: by that also, having bound the <s>khecarī</s> [<s>mudrā</s>], one leaves this earthly plane.
8.84cd: Then, having consecrated grains with the great weapon-<s>mantra</s> twenty-one times,
8.85ab: one should cast [them] in all directions, visualizing [them] as blazing like fire;
8.85cd: meditating on that dwelling as free from obstacles, having withdrawn [the protection], one should direct it toward the <s>Īśa</s> (northeast) direction.
8.86ab: Then the wise one should prepare the five cow-products (<s>pañcagavya</s>) with the five faces:
8.86cd: cow urine, cow dung, milk, yogurt, and ghee.
8.87ab: One should consecrate [these] with the six limbs up to the highest; [and prepare] <s>kuśa</s>-grass water;
8.87cd: having bound the <s>mudrā</s>, [producing] the nectar of the substances, one should contemplate its <s>tattva</s> (essential nature).
8.88ab: With that, one should sprinkle the ground; store a small amount separately;
8.88cd: then one should perform the site-worship (<s>vāstuyāga</s>) through the utterance of <s>Mālinī</s>.
8.89ab: Filling the cupped hands with flowers, one should utter [the <s>mantra</s>] beginning with 'pha';
8.89cd: meditating on the path ending with <s>Śakti</s>, one should cast [the flowers] at [the syllable] ending with 'na.'
8.90ab: Having offered fragrance, incense, and so forth, one should worship <s>Gaṇeśa</s> (Lord of the Hosts);
8.90cd: placing the seat of six layers, then above it with <s>praṇava</s> (Oṃ).
8.91ab: With [the <s>mantra</s>] 'gāṃ,' one should worship the Lord of Obstacles (<s>Vighneśa</s>) with fragrance, flowers, and the rest;
8.91cd: his limbs [are formed] with the syllable 'ga' combined with the six long vowels.
8.92ab: Visualizing [him as] three-eyed, joyful, elephant-faced, dwarf-formed,
8.92cd: one who desires <s>siddhi</s> should release [<s>Gaṇeśa</s>] and then worship the great weapon-<s>mantra</s>.
8.93ab: Having offered <s>Ananta</s> (the Infinite), <s>Dharma</s> (Righteousness), <s>Jñāna</s> (Knowledge), <s>Vairāgya</s> (Dispassion),
8.93cd: and <s>Aiśvarya</s> (Sovereignty), and the pistil (<s>karṇikā</s>)—this is the seat of six layers.
8.94ab: Above this, one should place [the deity], visualizing [him as] bearing sword and shield,
8.94cd: fearsome, with great fangs, exceedingly terrible, with furrowed brows,
8.95ab: endowed with his own group of six limbs, surrounded by the <s>Mātṛs</s> of the directions;
8.95cd: the six limbs [formed] with his own vowels, blazing with the syllable 'phaṭ.'
8.96ab: Visualizing [the deity] in that very form, one should then worship the eight <s>Mātṛs</s> (Mothers):
8.96cd: <s>Indrāṇī</s> on the eastern petal—one should visualize [her] simultaneously with the <s>vajra</s> (thunderbolt);
8.97ab: <s>Āgneyī</s> bearing a spear in hand; <s>Yāmyā</s> bearing a staff; then
8.97cd: <s>Nairṛtī</s>, <s>Vāruṇānī</s>, and <s>Vāyavī</s>—the discerning one
8.98ab: should visualize [them] simultaneously, O beloved, equipped with sword, noose, and banner;
8.98cd: <s>Kauverī</s> bearing a mace; <s>Īśānī</s> equipped with a trident.
8.99ab: Having worshipped with fragrance, flowers, and the rest, one should perform the fire-offering (<s>homa</s>) according to one's own <s>Tantra</s>.
8.99cd: First, one should prepare the water-vessel (<s>kalaśa</s>), consecrated with a thousand [recitations].
8.100ab: One should offer a thousand oblations therein; then, having recited, one should release [the deity].
8.100cd: After [reciting] one hundred and eight complete [times], one should begin the worship.
8.101ab: Therein first, taking a pot (<s>kumbha</s>) made of copper or other [metal], without cracks,
8.101cd: containing all <s>mantra</s>-herbs within, filled with fragrant water,
8.102ab: with a mango-leaf spout, with a garland and thread and white necklace,
8.102cd: marked with a demon-destroying <s>tilaka</s>, covered with a pair of white cloths,
8.103ab: consecrated with one hundred and eight recitations, worshipped with the root <s>mantra</s>;
8.103cd: the subsidiary vessels (<s>vārdhānī</s>) should likewise be of similar nature, but worshipped with the weapon-<s>mantra</s>.
8.104ab: Having offered a sprinkling as the previously described seat, the discerning one
8.104cd: should then worship <s>Indra</s> and the other [guardians] in their respective directions with the proclaimed vowels.
8.105ab: Then one should pour an unbroken stream from the subsidiary vessel;
8.105cd: then rotate the pot; then address the world-protectors thus:
8.106ab: 'O <s>Śakra</s> (Indra)! By you, in your own direction, for the pacification of obstacles,
8.106cd: [you] must remain attentive until the end of the rite, by the command of <s>Śiva</s>.'
8.107ab: Having first brought [the pot] there, one should place the embodied vessel on the seat;
8.107cd: on its southern side, one should place the subsidiary vessel.
8.108ab: The knower of <s>mantras</s>, having placed [the deities] from the self-form onward to the end of worship in the pot,
8.108cd: having worshipped with fragrance, flowers, and the rest, should worship this [deity] in the subsidiary vessel.
8.109ab: The discerning one, having made a <s>maṇḍala</s> (diagram) with fragrances at the seat of <s>Brahmā</s>,
8.109cd: should worship therein the sixfold, threefold, or even single [arrangement].
8.110ab: The scraping of the fire-pit, its drawing, pounding, and plastering,
8.110cd: the crossroads-enclosure, and the establishment of the <s>vajra</s>,
8.111ab: and the arrangement of <s>kuśa</s>-grass lining, border, and mats—
8.111cd: all should be done with the weapon-<s>mantra</s>; one should place the <s>Vidyā</s> [with] 'Oṃ hrīṃ.'
8.112ab: Having placed <s>Śiva</s> with 'Oṃ' and having worshipped the pair again,
8.112cd: one should bring the sacred fire (<s>Jātavedas</s>) in a copper vessel or earthen plate.
8.113ab: Meditating [on the fire] as the seed of <s>Śiva</s>, one should cast [it] into the womb of <s>Vidyā</s>;
8.113cd: then the five invocations (<s>āhuti</s>)—one should offer with the <s>vidyā</s>-limbs alone.
8.114ab: Then the rite beginning with generation (<s>janana</s>)—all is accomplished when thus performed;
8.114cd: meditating on <s>Parāparā</s>, one should again offer the full oblation (<s>pūrṇāhuti</s>).
8.115ab: Having worshipped the mother and father of the fire, one should release [them];
8.115cd: then, having extracted the fire again for the accomplishment of the chewing [rite] and other practices,
8.116ab: or, from a blazing fire, one should draw the flame with the left [nostril] wind (<s>vāyu</s>),
8.116cd: hold [it] in the heart with <s>kumbhaka</s> (breath retention), and cast [it] forth again with the right [nostril].
8.117ab: One should offer the full [oblation] as before—this is another method of the <s>Śiva</s>-fire;
8.117cd: beholding that [fire] as <s>Śiva</s>'s form, the inner visualization of its Self [proceeds] in sequence.
8.118ab: The <s>mantra</s>-practitioner should perform the inner visualization; then begin the fire-offering.
8.118cd: Having satiated the root [<s>mantra</s>] with a hundred [oblations], then its six limbs with the six-limb [<s>mantras</s>],
8.119ab: the remaining classes of <s>mantras</s> [should receive] oblations of one-tenth.
8.119cd: Then one should admit the disciples—pure, bathed, having fasted.
8.120ab: Having bowed to the God of gods, in the fourfold [arrangement] in sequence,
8.120cd: one should give the five cow-products (<s>pañcagavya</s>), the sacrificial porridge (<s>caru</s>), and the tooth-cleaning stick.
8.121ab: The porridge is perfected with the heart-[<s>mantra</s>], with sacrificial milk and rice;
8.121cd: the mixture (<s>saṃpāta</s>) [consecrated] with seven <s>mantras</s>; then divided into six portions
8.122ab: for <s>Śiva</s>, the fire, the <s>guru</s>, the disciples, the subsidiary vessel, and the pot, equally.
8.122cd: Then one should give the tooth-stick, born from a milk-tree;
8.123ab: its falling [direction] is auspicious if toward the east, north, northeast, or upward;
8.123cd: inauspicious in other [directions]—in that case also, a fire-offering of one hundred and eight [oblations] should be performed.
8.124ab: Then the preceptor should perform the external rite in all directions:
8.124cd: 'Oṃ kṣaḥ kṣaḥ to all beings <s>svāhā</s>'—with this <s>mantra</s>.
8.125ab: Having sipped water, having performed <s>nyāsa</s>, having worshipped <s>Śaṅkara</s> (Śiva),
8.125cd: [one should retire] to the dwelling, pure.
8.126ab: Having performed <s>nyāsa</s> for the disciples and for oneself especially,
8.126cd: in the morning, having done the daily rites, one should interpret the dreams.
8.127ab: One should reveal to them what is auspicious; for inauspicious [dreams], one should perform a fire-offering.
8.127cd: Then [one should note the omens of] flowers, fruits, and the like; well-adorned women wearing ornaments;
8.128ab: crossing over calamity; descending to an auspicious place;
8.128cd: drinking wine; severing a head; eating raw meat;
8.129ab: seeing a deity in person; and anointing with excrement—
8.129cd: having seen such auspicious [dreams], one attains the desired <s>siddhi</s>.
8.130ab: The very same [visions], when [appearing] otherwise, are declared as bad dreams;
8.130cd: eating cooked meat, oil-massage, falling into a pit, and the like.
8.131ab: Having performed the atonement prescribed in the <s>Tantra</s>, then the conferring of twice-born status;
8.131cd: the wise one should always perform worship of the deity, fire, <s>guru</s>, and goddess.
8.132ab: Without offering to these, one should not eat anything at all;
8.132cd: one should avoid property belonging to the deity, property of the <s>guru</s>, and property of <s>Caṇḍī</s>.
8.133ab: The knower of <s>mantras</s> should not engage in fruitless activity even for a moment;
8.133cd: one should be devoted to the practice of <s>yoga</s> or to the practice of <s>mantras</s>.
8.134ab: Having thus instructed in these and other observances (<s>samaya</s>), one should release
8.134cd: the God of gods; then bathing of the disciples and oneself
8.135ab: should be performed with the <s>Śiva</s>-pot, which removes all evil deeds;
8.135cd: thus has this conventional (<s>sāmaya</s>) rite been described in brief.

(13.1) Now I shall explain this concentration on water (<s>vāruṇī dhāraṇā</s>),
by which, with yoga perfected, one becomes the lord of all that is within water.
(13.2) The yogin should meditate on the body as situated within water — white, cool, and perfectly round,
both externally and internally — and should think 'there is nothing else.'
(13.3) For one who practices thus, after seven days moisture arises;
freed from diseases of bile (<s>pitta</s>), he surely becomes [healthy] within a month.
(13.4) With unctuous limbs, with unctuous gaze, with dark curly hair,
he becomes a lord of yogins within a year; within three years, he rains like a cloud.
(13.5) This has been declared the first pure concentration on water (<s>vāruṇī</s>);
now I shall explain this same [concentration] again, distinguished by its varieties.
(13.6) As before, one should meditate on one's own white body, with activity,
situated above water, O Goddess, with the inner self absorbed in that.
(13.7) Within seven days one is freed from all diseases arising from bile;
after six months, stability arises, if one has attained oneness with that.
(13.8) Knowledge of the water-covering (<s>jalāvaraṇa</s>) arises for him within three years;
in the variety without activity as well, one becomes like <s>Varuṇa</s> everywhere.
(13.9) He reaches the water-principle (<s>vāruṇa tattva</s>); one should practice the stages (<s>bhūmikā</s>) progressively.
As before, [meditating on] the self as twelve finger-widths, situated in the middle of the throat,
(13.10) remembering the lord of the water-principle, one beholds [him] surely before long;
that vision becomes stable when the fifth [stage] in one's own nature is firm.
(13.11) When the dual variety also becomes firm — [like] a moon-disc covered by clouds —
one attains equality with that; then [the vision becomes] of the full form (<s>sakalarūpiṇī</s>);
(13.12) one meditates, filling the body with white-colored radiance;
that itself becomes stable; when [one] becomes well-established there,
(13.13) one should then meditate on [a vision] resembling the moon-disc freed from clouds;
one attains lordship over that; in the second [stage], one reaches stability.
(13.14) Then one should meditate on the white radiance illuminating below;
one attains the state of lord of <s>Vidyā</s>s (<s>Vidyeśvara</s>), born of the water-covering.
(13.15) When meditating on the white radiance pervading one's own body and situated there,
one attains sovereignty over all; when [firmly] established there, [it is] supremely firm.
(13.16) Equality with the meditated principle [arises] when the three stages are stable;
and when the second pair [of stages is stable], the awareness of its lord (<s>Īśāna</s>) arises.
(13.17) In the second pair elsewhere, the knower of yoga becomes equal to that and firm;
in the group of six, one attains lordship over all; but in the second pair elsewhere, there is a fall.
(13.18) Thus this procedure, applicable to all principles in the fifteen-fold division,
should be known by those who know the rites — it bestows the attainment of the fivefold fruit.
(13.19) Whatever other fruit has been stated from this, and will be stated [hereafter],
should be known as an incidental fruit — all of it, without deliberation.
(13.20) Thus has been declared the water-concentration with fifteen varieties;
for the accomplishment of yoga by yogins, now hear the fire [concentration] (<s>āgneyī</s>).
(13.21) One should meditate on the body as triangular, bearing rows of red flames;
fire risen from one's own body, blazing, indeed burning everything.
(13.22) Within seven days, O Goddess, sharpness arises in him;
within a month, the practitioner is freed from all [diseases] arising from wind and phlegm.
(13.23) He becomes sleepless, a great eater, producing little excrement and urine;
at will he burns whatever object is touched, from the season's end.
(13.24) After three years, having become equal to fire, he plays as fire does, at will;
when angered, he burns everything — mountains, forests, and groves.
(13.25) One should meditate on the self as mounted on a triangular <s>maṇḍala</s>,
with the distinction of activity and so on, everywhere, the discerning one.
(13.26) After seven days, free from diseases; after six months, one becomes like fire;
after three years, one beholds the complete fire-principle.
(13.27) Whatever is seen in the division of powers — that becomes stable in that division.
As before, meditating on the self as flame-like, situated in the middle of the palate,
(13.28) one beholds the lords of the fire-principle, all of them, in succession;
resembling a fire enveloped by smoke, shaped like a sun-disc,
(13.29) meditating from the middle of that, one reaches equality with the lords of the fire-principle;
with its mass of darkness struck by radiance, shining like smokeless fire,
(13.30) there one should meditate on the entire [form] — one would attain lordship over that;
like the radiance of daytime fire — there one should meditate on the radiance;
(13.31) one attains lordship over its mantras; having become well-established there,
one should manifest radiance there like a blazing jewel-lamp;
(13.32) the yogin, having become one with that, attains lordship over the lord of mantras;
meditating on the radiance both externally and internally, absorbed in that everywhere,
(13.33) he does not fall from that station until the dissolution, unbroken;
but at the dissolution, he reaches the supreme, peaceful station of <s>Śiva</s>.
(13.34) Thus the fire-concentration has been described in fifteen ways.
One should meditate on one's body as dark, round, marked with six dots,
(13.35) moving, with a whistling sound — [this is for one] devoted to the wind-concentration (<s>vāyavī dhāraṇā</s>).
From the cutting off of diseases born of phlegm, one becomes wind-like with [its] movement.
(13.36) The yogin should practice for six months, with the inner self absorbed in that;
having traveled a hundred <s>yojana</s>s, he returns in a <s>muhūrta</s> without fatigue.
(13.37) After three years, he assumes the very form of wind directly;
he pulverizes masses of mountains and uproots trees.
(13.38) When angered, he shakes <s>Indra</s> himself, with his servants, army, and vehicle.
One should meditate on one's own body as dark like collyrium;
(13.39) one attains all that was described before, within six months — there is no doubt;
after three years, having become one with it, one beholds the wind-principle.
(13.40) Between the eyebrows, one should remember one's form as collyrium-like;
one beholds all the lords of the wind-principle, swift as they are.
(13.41) Meditating on [a vision] resembling an indranīla (sapphire) covered by clouds, shaped like a sun-disc,
one reaches equality with that when one becomes merged therein.
(13.42) There one should meditate on the entire [form], resembling a split indranīla;
one attains lordship over its mantras, and then lordship over it as well.
(13.43) When meditating on that color pervading everything, upon the radiance, one attains
invincibility there, in that [radiance] flowing below and above.
(13.44) Thus has been declared this divine concentration born of wind.
Having meditated on one's body as wind-like, then remembering its absence —
(13.45) within seven days the yogin attains voidness (<s>śūnyatā</s>);
within a month, even if bitten by the king of serpents, he is not bewildered.
(13.46) Freed from all diseases, free from wrinkles and gray hair,
after six months, becoming like the sky, he passes even through subtle openings.
(13.47) After three years, he becomes directly like space itself;
by will alone, he becomes of enormous body, or of subtle body, likewise by will.
(13.48) Uncuttable and unpierceable, he sees holes in the earth,
[and his own body becomes like] the interior of a mine left over from the essence of a hundred flowers.
(13.49) After three years of meditating on the body [as space], knowledge of space arises;
and all the previously mentioned fruits from seven days onward, one obtains.
(13.50) On the forehead, one should likewise meditate, extending twelve finger-widths;
one sees all the lords of that principle, in succession, stationed before [oneself].
(13.51) Meditating [on a vision] resembling a moon-disc seized by <s>Rāhu</s>, one reaches equality with that;
one should meditate on the complete [form], like a moon-disc, stationed there.
(13.52) One attains lordship over its mantras; and by the moonlight, the status of <s>Indra</s> as well;
by that same [moonlight] flowing downward, both externally and internally, the wise one
(13.53) attains lordship over the lord of mantra-lords, and obtains incomparable knowledge;
and by that moonlight flowing upward, of the nature of nectar (<s>amṛta</s>),
(13.54) having attained independence, one is never obstructed anywhere.
Thus the concentrations on the five elements (<s>tattva</s>) have been fully declared.
(13.55) Awareness established in the pure [elements] and higher — the possession by the elements (<s>bhūtāveśa</s>) is fivefold here;
fixing the mind on those very [concentrations], [there is] the destruction of poisons and the like for oneself.
(13.56) And in any other awareness too, in whichever one desires by one's own will,
one should firmly fix the mind; by that, one enjoys its fruit.
(13.57) Even this one [concentration], being practiced, from the intermediate subdivisions,
may become an obstacle; one should not make one's abode there.
(13.58) Those who make their abode there will not attain the supreme fruit.
When the fivefold concentration is accomplished — the eightfold qualities beginning with [power over] <s>Piśāca</s>s
(13.59) and the five [powers] ending with <s>Aindra</s> are achieved for the yogins, even variously.
The desired fifteen stages, practicing in due sequence —
(13.60) after three years, one accomplishes the first; the next in two [years]; another in one;
after six months, another in five [months], four, and three [months],
(13.61) in two, in one, in a fortnight, in ten days, in five days,
in three, in two, and then in one [day]. For one whose desire is distributed, the order is as before.
(13.62) One attains the eternal station, having enjoyed the desired attainment.

18.1ab: Listen, O <s>Devī</s> (Goddess), to the supreme secret, unattainable by those whose selves are unprepared,
18.1cd: that which has not been told to anyone — that I shall now tell you.
18.2ab: Abandoning everything else, one should fix the mind here;
18.2cd: one should not worship a <s>liṅga</s> (emblem) made of clay, stone, metal, jewels, or the like.
18.3ab: One should worship the inner <s>liṅga</s> (<s>ādhyātmika liṅga</s>), in which all that is moving and unmoving is absorbed;
18.3cd: for the liṅga-nature of the external <s>liṅga</s> is established by this [inner one].
18.4ab: Therefore, one should worship this [inner liṅga], having taken refuge in supreme non-duality (<s>paramādvaita</s>);
18.4cd: through supreme meditation (<s>anudhyāna</s>), O Mistress of the Gods, by means of the supreme atom (<s>paramāṇu</s>).
18.5ab: He who has been thus meditated upon — he alone sees this <s>liṅga</s>, no other;
18.5cd: that which is called pulsation (<s>spandana</s>), situated in the heart.
18.6ab: Having fixed the mind there, trembling and arising [occur];
18.6cd: there the knower of <s>yoga</s> attains tranquility within a single month.
18.7ab: The <s>liṅga</s> that has risen from the heart up to the <s>brahmarandhra</s> (crown aperture), O <s>Īśvarī</s> (Sovereign Goddess),
18.7cd: illuminating the entire body to its extremities by its own radiance, of stainless splendor —
18.8ab: there the great-minded one sees the entire web of <s>mantra</s>s;
18.8cd: having ascended to its summit, for one month only, with undivided attention,
18.9ab: then, when that is well accomplished, within six months all <s>siddhi</s>s (accomplishments) [are attained].
18.9cd: Without knowing this <s>liṅga</s>, whoever, though bearing a liṅga, resorts to [an external] liṅga,
18.10ab: his effort is in vain — he does not obtain the fruit of the <s>liṅga</s>.
18.10cd: This great <s>liṅga</s> of <s>Śiva</s> is [not] accomplished in the self-liṅga.
18.11ab: When this [inner liṅga] is accomplished, the liṅga-bearer, like a liṅga, abiding in the liṅga yet free from the liṅga,
18.11cd: becomes [one with it] — so what [other] support [is needed]? By this, one is marked by the liṅga (<s>liṅgaliṅgita</s>).
18.12ab: When the <s>yogī</s>, with this liṅga-mark, goes outward,
18.12cd: then he is to be known as a liṅga-bearer (<s>liṅgī</s>); the liṅga is held to extend to the end of the body (<s>purānta</s>).
18.13ab: Through this knowledge of the <s>liṅga</s>, <s>yogī</s>s are said to be marked [by it];
18.13cd: the <s>mantra</s>s, presided over by this [liṅga], according to the distinctions of peaceful (<s>śānta</s>), fierce (<s>raudra</s>), and so forth,
18.14ab: become [effective] — what wonder is there in that for one whose mind has entered that state?
18.14cd: If, having resorted to the fierce (<s>raudra</s>) disposition, one practices <s>yoga</s>,
18.15ab: one becomes impossible to gaze upon by all — gods, <s>asura</s>s, and humans;
18.15cd: free from coming and going, seeing all, fearless.
18.16ab: For as long as one remains [thus] even for a <s>muhūrta</s> (moment), for that long one attains the Lord;
18.16cd: [thus] possessed, one sees everything, with the splendor of ten million suns.
18.17ab: That which is the imperishable, the unmanifest, called <s>Śaiva</s> (pertaining to <s>Śiva</s>) and <s>Bhairava</s> —
18.17cd: having seen that, within half a year, the <s>yogī</s> would attain omniscience.
18.18ab: He who, having reached this [state], attains satisfaction,
18.18cd: and does not [resort] to artificial <s>yoga</s>s — he is liberated from all bonds.
18.19ab: <s>Yoga</s>s that employ <s>prāṇāyāma</s> (breath control) and such marks are considered artificial;
18.19cd: therefore they do not merit a sixteenth part of this uncreated [yoga].
18.20ab: Practicing this, the <s>yogī</s> sees divine signs;
18.20cd: the <s>yogī</s>, seated upright, should not think of anything at all.
18.21ab: Within a <s>muhūrta</s>, he burns everything in the body, both the uncreated and the created;
18.21cd: as it burns, there arises the experience of trembling.
18.22ab: Then, when that [state] becomes stable, a light shines within;
18.22cd: having seen that supreme radiance, divine knowledge unfolds.
18.23ab: One attains the state of independent <s>Śiva</s>-hood even while enjoying sense objects;
18.23cd: with the divine eye unblinking, for as long as one remains even a moment.
18.24ab: From that, one realizes the all-pervading nature of the Self;
18.24cd: one should cultivate that very [state] with effort, desiring the fruit of all <s>siddhi</s>s.
18.25ab: Then the <s>yogī</s>, trembling, should cultivate that [state] with great gentleness;
18.25cd: thereupon one perceives a radiance at the tip of the forehead on all sides.
18.26ab: Having seen that supreme radiance, one would attain divine knowledge;
18.26cd: within six months effortlessly, within a year it is fully accomplished.
18.27ab: Having become equal in power to <s>Śiva</s>, one goes wherever one desires;
18.27cd: the knower of <s>yoga</s>, having made the mind all-pervading, within a mere <s>muhūrta</s>,
18.28ab: attains the possession of <s>Śakti</s> (<s>śāktāveśa</s>), characterized by the experience of trembling;
18.28cd: then, when that [state] becomes stable, within a single month, the knower of <s>yoga</s>
18.29ab: perceives the radiance of <s>Śakti</s>, stable both externally and internally;
18.29cd: when that is well accomplished, [one perceives] that which arises from all the senses, with care.
18.30ab: There one clearly attains unobstructed knowledge,
18.30cd: and here it should be understood as all-pervading, that which is connected with the object of the senses.
18.31ab: Thus this <s>tattva</s> (reality) has been declared according to different stages;
18.31cd: having completely abandoned the action organs (<s>karmendriya</s>s) up to and including the intellect (<s>buddhi</s>),
18.32ab: the discerning one should meditate on the supreme <s>Śakti</s> everywhere;
18.32cd: having made the mind motionless, until one becomes absorbed in that [Śakti],
18.33ab: one attains the all-pervading state in a mere instant;
18.33cd: having burned the net of bonds (<s>pāśa</s>s), one would obtain the desired fruit.
18.34ab: Therefore, one should practice this, having made a firm resolve within oneself,
18.34cd: [contemplating] where the individual soul (<s>jīva</s>), freed from support, will attain dissolution (<s>laya</s>).
18.35ab: That place is held to be the origin-field of all <s>mantra</s>s;
18.35cd: that should be known as twofold, according to the distinction of external and internal.
18.36ab: That which has gone beyond the measure of qualification — that is known as the bestower of all <s>siddhi</s>s;
18.36cd: or alternatively, as [the mind of] one who is departing, the discerning one, by means of the dream-state (<s>svapnavṛtti</s>),
18.37ab: should effect cessation (<s>nirodha</s>) at the central place for a mere instant;
18.37cd: there one perceives the power of consciousness (<s>cicchakti</s>), momentary yet undivided.
18.38ab: That alone is the supreme reality (<s>tattva</s>); from it this world has arisen;
18.38cd: that itself is the <s>mantra</s>-body, according to the <s>Siddhayogīśvarī</s> doctrine.
18.39ab: The <s>mantra</s>s, embraced by that [power], bestow the fruit of all <s>siddhi</s>s;
18.39cd: one whose phonemes are slightly altered, free from what is to be rejected and what is to be adopted,
18.40ab: the awareness that one attains — that is called the <s>Śiva</s>-reality (<s>śivatattva</s>);
18.40cd: by making the mind stable there, one would attain omniscience.
18.41ab: There indeed one sees divine signs, without doubt;
18.41cd: wherever in the body a transformation arises,
18.42ab: preceded by intention (<s>saṃkalpa</s>), O <s>Devī</s>, that is the reality, the supreme reality;
18.42cd: the great <s>yogī</s> should practice that with the desire for omniscience.
18.43ab: One attains the supreme state, having enjoyed the desired <s>siddhi</s>;
18.43cd: the <s>yogī</s> should always worship himself with care, with fragrance, flowers, and the like,
18.44ab: at the region of the <s>brahmarandhra</s> (crown aperture), or even mentally;
18.44cd: through contact with liquid substances, bathing (<s>snapana</s>) of that [inner liṅga] takes place.
18.45ab: The apprehension of the fragrance of incense, flowers, and the like is considered worship (<s>yajana</s>);
18.45cd: the tasting of the six flavors (<s>ṣaḍrasa</s>) is designated as its food offering (<s>naivedya</s>).
18.46ab: Whatever phoneme (<s>varṇa</s>) one utters — that is proclaimed as <s>japa</s> (recitation);
18.46cd: having fixed the mind there, on the substance that is burning,
18.47ab: as long as it remains in the state of blazing — for that long the fire offering (<s>homa</s>) is performed;
18.47cd: whatever form one perceives — that alone is considered meditation (<s>dhyāna</s>).
18.48ab: Incidentally, this great non-dual worship (<s>advaitayajana</s>) has been declared;
18.48cd: with radiance like the rising sun, the mind [held] steady at the upper aperture (<s>ūrdhvadvāra</s>),
18.49ab: or in the heart — one should do likewise; or at the <s>dvādaśānta</s> (twelve-finger point above the head), one may attain [it];
18.49cd: then, within half a month, that form is perceived.
18.50ab: Having perceived it, by practicing that, one becomes fit for omniscience;
18.50cd: the <s>yogī</s>, covering the face with a cloth, should direct [the mind] to the target.
18.51ab: From below the bulb of the navel (<s>nābhikanda</s>) up to the reality [that resides] above the crown (<s>śikhā</s>),
18.51cd: resembling a subtle star, made fierce with rays of flame,
18.52ab: at the end of the <s>prāṇa</s>-power, one sees one's own form;
18.52cd: that very [form], through practice, O <s>Devī</s>, undergoes expansion.
18.53ab: That is taught as the face of all <s>mantra</s>s in all <s>tantra</s>s;
18.53cd: then, within a month, a certain awareness (<s>saṃvitti</s>) is attained,
18.54ab: by which one knows everything established in the heart;
18.54cd: having known that, the <s>yogī</s> should not properly impart it to just anyone.
18.55ab: One should teach [only] from a chapter [at a time]; [otherwise] one may meet untimely death;
18.55cd: even after death, in the mass of graves, one is gradually consumed.
18.56ab: Thus having known, O <s>Mahādevī</s> (Great Goddess), one should cultivate one's own welfare;
18.56cd: a disciple who receives [the teaching] improperly falls into hell.
18.57ab: And he does not attain that [fruit] in time — my word is never untrue;
18.57cd: having obtained knowledge through the proper method, afterward one does not fall.
18.58ab: Then the wise one should perform the removal of [that disciple's] knowledge;
18.58cd: having meditated on him and placed [him] before [oneself], the knower of <s>yoga</s>, in his own form,
18.59ab: should lay out the sixfold path in that one's body as previously described;
18.59cd: then, seeing that flame, from the tip of that one's thumb, gradually,
18.60ab: one should lead the collected radiance to the <s>dvādaśānta</s> with undivided attention;
18.60cd: then, contemplating that one there as having become one with <s>Śiva</s>,
18.61ab: one should meditate there on a form of darkness, whose nature is concealment (<s>tirobhāvana</s>);
18.61cd: descending along that path, having arrived at the tip of the thumb,
18.62ab: one should meditate [on it] both externally and internally, with the radiance of dense collyrium;
18.62cd: by this procedure, for that deluded, evil-minded one,
18.63ab: knowledge, <s>mantra</s>s, <s>vidyā</s>s and the like do not perform any benefit;
18.63cd: through a mere intention of the mind, even for one not [previously] seen, it takes effect;
18.64ab: [and] for one somehow perceived, who constantly causes harm;
18.64cd: or alternatively, having meditated on a form resembling the solar disc placed before [oneself],
18.65ab: one should contemplate it seized by a power in the form of <s>Svarbhānu</s> (eclipse-demon <s>Rāhu</s>);
18.65cd: but [this should be done] only when [the target] has committed thousands of offenses, with great anger;
18.66ab: one should perform this procedure only in sport, never otherwise;
18.66cd: by this procedure, one fallen from knowledge, and by yet another [method],
18.67ab: cannot be joined [to knowledge] again, until he is uplifted by that very [teacher];
18.67cd: but one whose mind is drawn by compassion, having performed purification for that one,
18.68ab: through intense <s>prāṇāyāma</s> and other prescribed expiations (<s>prāyaścitta</s>),
18.68cd: should then perform initiation (<s>dīkṣā</s>) for that one by the previously described path.
18.69ab: Then one obtains all fruit; therefore, with undivided attention,
18.69cd: having thus known, the wise one should with effort seek out a <s>guru</s> (teacher),
18.70ab: from whom satisfaction arises, of the nature of the nectar of <s>Śiva</s>-knowledge;
18.70cd: one should not investigate his conduct, whether auspicious or inauspicious.
18.71ab: He alone knows that — whether it is appropriate or not;
18.71cd: when he is attached to improper actions that destroy the two <s>prāṇa</s>s,
18.72ab: then he should be restrained by the [disciple] who has bowed down, who is wise;
18.72cd: but even when strongly restrained by that one, if he does not turn back,
18.73ab: then, having gone elsewhere, one should contemplate <s>Śiva</s> alone;
18.73cd: this rule of <s>yoga</s> has been declared in brief, for the benefit of <s>yogī</s>s.
18.74ab: Here there is no purity, nor impurity, no consideration of what may or may not be eaten,
18.74cd: neither duality nor non-duality, nor worship of the <s>liṅga</s> and such;
18.75ab: nor is there abandonment of that, nor possessionlessness,
18.75cd: nor possession, [nor] the collection of matted hair, ashes, and the like;
18.76ab: nor abandonment of that, nor the observance or non-observance of vows and the like,
18.76cd: nor entering sacred places and the like, nor upholding commitments (<s>samaya</s>) and the like;
18.77ab: [nor] the external form, liṅga-marks, and the like, nor name and lineage and the like —
18.77cd: in this [yoga], nothing whatsoever is enjoined, nor is anything prohibited.
18.78ab: Everything here is [both] enjoined and prohibited;
18.78cd: however, this alone, O Mistress of the Gods, is prescribed as a rule here:
18.79ab: the mind must be made stable in the reality (<s>tattva</s>), with great effort, by the <s>yogī</s>;
18.79cd: and whatever that [tattva] may be for whomever, one should practice accordingly.
18.80ab: One whose mind is steady in the <s>tattva</s>, even while enjoying sense objects,
18.80cd: is not touched by faults, like a lotus petal by water.
18.81ab: One fortified with poison-removing <s>mantra</s>s and the like, even while consuming
18.81cd: poison, is not deluded by it — so too the great-minded <s>yogī</s>.
18.82ab: Thus this has been declared, O <s>Devī</s> — what else do you wish to ask?
